## DEVICE NOTE (2026-08-27) — GPU is a different realization, not a bit-copy

With identical weights and inputs (verified: weight diff 0.0, TF32 off), CPU
and CUDA runs differ because `nn.Dropout` draws its masks from device-specific
RNG streams: 4.9% max relative in the first train-mode forward pass, 1% in the
3-step forecast; eval-mode forward passes agree to float32. `MORTCAL_DEVICE`
therefore changes the realization of every dropout-trained family (NLC, CNN,
LSTM, NB). RULE: one device per regime, recorded in the parquet (`device`
column should be added to runner rows before any GPU sweep); never mix.

## MEASURED 2026-08-27 evening — cache useless, GPU 5-8x, tonight on CPU

timings_cached ≈ timings_solo: the `_subset_cache` gives NO speed-up (kept,
harmless). GPU: NB 39.6→8.3 s, NLC 34.6→4.5, CNN 53.9→9.9, LSTM ~1x.
Shift+placebo run CPU-only (12 workers, 1 thread each) — 12 CUDA contexts do
not fit 4 GB VRAM and one serialized GPU is slower than 12 CPU workers for
the neural axis. GPU hybrid is for STABLE, after probing 2–3 concurrent GPU
processes. `device` is recorded per parquet row.

## SWEEP WORKERS NEED CPU TORCH (2026-08-27 evening)

12 workers importing the cu124 torch each map ~2 GB of CUDA DLLs ->
`OSError: [WinError 1455] The paging file is too small` (15.7 GB RAM, small
pagefile). Sweeps therefore run from `C:/Users/Gaming/venvs/mortcal-cpu`
(default deps + PyPI torch==2.6.0, which is the CPU build on Windows, +
gpytorch). The CUDA env `C:/Users/Gaming/venvs/mortcal` is for the GPU
hybrid on STABLE only, at low parallelism. Also: `Start-Process
-ArgumentList` joins elements WITHOUT quoting — launch bash via a wrapper
script in a space-free path (scratchpad/launch_detached.sh).

